# The one NPC in the harness that can fight.
#
# The tester cannot: its role is built on Template_Temple, whose attitude sensors cover Neutral and Friendly
# and which has no hostile branch, no attack and no combat. So <<attitude hostile>> on it changes a value
# nothing reads, and "does a hostile NPC behave like one" was unanswerable there.
#
# A goblin scavenger answers it. It is spawned calm -- its attitude to whoever spawned it is overridden to
# friendly -- or it would attack instead of talking. This dialogue is what makes it hostile, and only for as
# long as it takes to see.

npc: @harness_fighter
title: LowTalk harness (fighter)
speaker: Scavenger

== start
I can actually fight, which the tester cannot. My attitude to you is {attitude()}.
-> Turn on me for six seconds <<if not checked("attitude.behaviour")>>
    <<jump check_attitude_behaviour>>
-> Send me away
    <<jump leave>>
-> Nothing for now
    <<end>>

== check_attitude_behaviour
Stand back. Switch to creative first if you would rather not be hit.
<<attitude hostile>>
<<if attitude() == "hostile">>
    <<jump check_attitude_behaviour_ok>>
<<else>>
    <<jump check_attitude_behaviour_bad>>
<<endif>>

== check_attitude_behaviour_ok
Hostile now, and I mean it. Close this window and I go back to friendly, however it closes -- including me knocking you out of it. The question this answers is the one the tester could not: whether an NPC whose role has a hostile branch actually comes at you. If I just stand here, say so with /harness feedback.
-> Send me away
    <<jump leave>>
-> Stay there
    <<end>>

== check_attitude_behaviour_bad
Wrong, or the assertion is. attitude() reads {attitude()}. I have set myself friendly again either way.
-> Send me away
    <<jump leave>>
-> Stay there
    <<end>>

== leave
Right you are.
<<despawn>>
